              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Pasting schemes are one possibility to give a rigorous treatment of the (older) notational device of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pasting+diagrams"} pasting diagrams
          %h2#notions_of_pasting_scheme Notions of pasting scheme
          %p
            More than one definition of
            %em pasting scheme
            has been used to give a justification to the practice of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pasting+diagrams"} pasting diagrams
          %p
            For example, there are pasting schemes in the sense of
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Johnson1987"} Johnson 1987
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Power1990"} Power 1990
          %h3#powers_definition Power’s definition
          %p
            Power’s notion is based on planar embeddings of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quivers"} quivers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            that satisfy the following conditions:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
                is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite
                and
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+object"} connected
            %li
              %p
                For the free category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(Q)
                generated by
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Q
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endomorphism"} endomorphism
                is an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+map"} identity
                (
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
                has no directed cycles);
            %li
              %p
                There are vertices
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo ,
                      %mi t
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s, t
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
                (the
                %em source
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %em sink
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi v
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi s
                      %mo ,
                      %mi v
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(Q)(s, v)
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi v
                      %mo ,
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(Q)(v, t)
                are
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+set"} inhabited
                (there exist directed paths in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
                from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
                to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi v
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
                and from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi v
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
                to
                = succeed ")." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi t
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
          %p
            We will call such quivers
            = succeed "," do
              %em progressive
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagrams"} string diagrams
          %p
            There is a restricted
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+realization"} geometric realization
            functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Quiv
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ⇉
                      %mn 1
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi op
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi skel
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mi op
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi R
                  %mi Space
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Quiv = Set^{(0 \rightrightarrows 1)^{op}} \stackrel{skel}{\to} Set^{\Delta^{op}} \stackrel{R}{\to} Space
          %p
            (into a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convenient+category+of+topological+spaces"} convenient category of topological spaces
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Space
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo :
                    %mi Quiv
                    %mo →
                    %mi Space
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R: Quiv \to Space
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              %strong (after Power)
              A
              %em pasting scheme
              consists of a progressive
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver"} quiver
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
              together with an subspace embedding
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo :
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ↪
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i: R(Q) \hookrightarrow \mathbb{R}^2
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              is a
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^1
              embedding on each edge interior.
          %p
            To each pasting scheme
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Q
                  %mo ,
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Q, i)
            we may associate a 2-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computad"} computad
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo =
                    %mi Comp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Q
                    %mo ,
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C = Comp(Q, i)
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The underlying quiver of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                is
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Q
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            %li
              %p
                The elements of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_2
                (the 2-cells) are the bounded
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+components"} connected components
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mn 2
                        %mo ∖
                        %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi R
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Q
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^2 \setminus i(R(Q))
          %p
            To complete the description of the computad, we need to define the source and target
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi τ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Mor
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma(c), \tau(c) \in Mor(F(Q))
            of a 2-cell
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e \in Q_1
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi e
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i(e)
            lies in the boundary of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in e
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi i
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t(x) = i'(x)
            be the tangent vector, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n(x)
            the inward-pointing normal (pointing into
            = succeed ");" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^1
            embedding condition ensures that these make sense. Then say that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
            is a
            %em negative edge
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            if
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi det
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                      %mrow
                        %mi t
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo )
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \det \binom{t(x)}{n(x)} \lt 0
          %p
            (the cell
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            is “to the right” as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            is traversed along
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
            %em positive edge
            if
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi det
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                      %mrow
                        %mi t
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo )
                  %mo &gt;
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \det \binom{t(x)}{n(x)} \gt 0
          %p
            (the cell
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            is to the left).
          %p
            Power shows that under the progressivity conditions, for each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in C_2
            there are unique distinct vertices
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mi v
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u, v
            such that the negative edges of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            form a directed path
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \in F(Q)(u, v)
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            form a directed path
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi β
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi u
                    %mo ,
                    %mi v
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta \in F(Q)(u, v)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma(c) = \alpha
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi τ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi β
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau(c) = \beta
          %p
            %em Power’s pasting theorem
            may be stated in the following form:
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              For every pasting scheme
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Q
                      %mo ,
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Q, i)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Comp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Q
                    %mo ,
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Comp(Q, i)
              has exactly one 2-morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma
              whose
              = succeed "-domain" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
              = succeed "-codomain" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
              = succeed "-domain" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma
              is the directed path consisting of
              %strong positive
              edges of the unique unbounded component of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ∖
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^2 \setminus i(R(Q))
              = succeed "-codomain" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              %strong negative
              edges of that component.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pasting+diagram"} pasting diagram
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pasting+law"} pasting law
                for
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullbacks"} pullbacks
